### Purpose & Scope
These rules exist to:
1. Keep the code-base consistent, clean and maintainable.
2. Ensure modern Android/Kotlin best-practices are applied.
3. Provide clear guard-rails for LLM-assisted development.

All contributors (human or AI) **MUST** follow this document unless a deviation is explicitly approved in writing by a senior developer.

---
### Architecture Principles
• Clean Architecture with MVVM presentation layer.  
• Single-direction data flow: UI ↔ ViewModel ↔ UseCase ↔ Repository ↔ DataSource.  
• Business logic lives in *UseCase* classes.  
• Data access is abstracted through *Repository* interfaces with separate Remote & Local implementations.  
• Inject dependencies using **Hilt** (preferred) or **Dagger 2** (legacy parts).  
• UI components use **ViewBinding** or **Jetpack Compose** (new screens).

---
### Class & File Naming
| Layer | Suffix | Example |
|-------|--------|---------|
| Fragment | `Fragment` | `QuickScanFragment` |
| Activity | `Activity` | `MainActivity` |
| ViewModel | `ViewModel` | `QuickScanViewModel` |
| UseCase | `UseCase` | `CleanCacheUseCase` |
| Repository (interface) | `Repository` | `UserRepository` |
| Repository (impl) | `RepositoryImpl` | `UserRepositoryImpl` |
| Adapter | `Adapter` | `AppsListAdapter` |
| Utils (stateless) | `object` | `DateUtils` |

Variable / Method names → `camelCase`; booleans start with `is/has/should/can`; constants in `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` inside `companion object` or `object Constants`.

---
### Kotlin Coding Standards
• Explicit return types on all **public** functions & properties.  
• Prefer `val` over `var`; restrict mutability.  
• Default visibility is `private`; expose only what is required for tests or API.  
• Trailing commas on multi-line lists/maps/args.  
• Max line length **120** characters.  
• Keep functions < **50** lines; extract helpers otherwise.

---
### Concurrency (Coroutines)
• Use **structured concurrency** – launch coroutines only from a `CoroutineScope` tied to lifecycle (e.g., `viewModelScope`, `lifecycleScope`).  
• Dispatcher rules: `Dispatchers.IO` for blocking I/O, `Dispatchers.Default` for CPU heavy jobs, `Dispatchers.Main` for UI.  
• Always handle cancellation (e.g., `isActive` checks in long loops).  
• Prefer `suspend` functions over callbacks / Rx when adding new code.

---
### Error Handling & Logging
• Wrap risky operations with `try/catch`; never swallow exceptions silently – log or rethrow with context.  
• Use a central `Logger`/`CrashReporter` abstraction; no `println`/raw `Log.d` in production code.  
• Show user-friendly messages; never expose internal stack-traces.  
• Fail soft but *loud*: recover when possible, otherwise surface a controlled error state to UI.

---
### Resources & UI
• Resource naming:  
  • Layouts `[type]_[feature]` → `fragment_quick_scan`.  
  • Drawables `[category]_[description]` → `ic_premium_check`.  
  • IDs `[viewType][Description]` → `btnSubmit`.  
• Never hard-code dimensions or colors; use theme attributes & `dimens.xml`.  
• Support **dark mode** unless business decides otherwise.  
• Observe `viewLifecycleOwner` for LiveData/StateFlow to avoid leaks.  
• Cancel animations & clear bindings in `onDestroyView`.

---
### Permissions & Security
• Request only minimum required permissions and explain the rationale to users.  
• Check permission status before each sensitive operation.  
• Store sensitive data in encrypted preferences or Keystore; never plain text.  
• Validate user input & file paths to avoid injection / path traversal.

---
### Privacy & Data-Protection
• Collect analytics **only** after explicit consent (GDPR/CCPA).  
• Provide opt-out controls in Settings.  
• Use secure TLS connections for any network traffic.  
• Do not log PII.

---
### esting & Quality Assurance
• Unit tests for all UseCases & Repositories (happy + edge cases).  
• UI tests for critical flows (Espresso or Compose Test).  
• New code must reach ≥ **80 %** line coverage.  
• Fast tests run on every PR; slow instrumentation tests at least once before release.

---
### Analytics & Monetization
• Event naming: `[screen]_[action]_[element]` (snake_case).  
• Always put analytics for important logic flows and errors

---
### Documentation & Comments
• Document public APIs with **KDoc**.  
• Inline comments focus on *why*, not what (use `// Reason:`).  
• Keep docs up-to-date; outdated comments are worse than none.

---
### Localization & Accessibility
• All user-visible strings in `strings.xml`; mark non-translatables.  
• Test UI with long translations and RTL locales.  
• Provide content descriptions for interactive elements; pass accessibility checks.

---
### Performance
• Apply lazy loading & pagination for large lists.  
• Avoid memory leaks – no long-lived references to `Context`/`View`.  
• Measure with **Android Profiler** before premature optimisation.  
• Respect battery & background execution limits; use `WorkManager` for deferrable tasks.

---
### Dependency Management & Updates
• Declare libraries in **Gradle Version Catalog** (or central `build.gradle`).  
• Monitor for CVE/security updates and apply patch versions quickly.  
• Remove unused libraries to keep APK thin.

---
### LLM-Assistant Ground Rules
• AI must not invent APIs – only use existing or newly-added dependencies.  
• Ask clarifying questions when context is insufficient.  
• Keep edits minimal, scoped and 100 % compilable.  
• Never leak private keys, tokens or sensitive data in chat or code.

---
### Review Checklist (TL;DR)
1. ❑ Follows architectural layers & naming.  
2. ❑ Null-safety & error-handling in place.  
3. ❑ UI uses binding/Compose, no leaks.  
4. ❑ Coroutine scope/cancellation handled.  
5. ❑ No hard-coded strings, colors or dimensions.  
6. ❑ Unit/UI tests added or updated.  
7. ❑ Documentation updated.  
8. ❑ Security/privacy considerations addressed.